# Infrastructure as Code — {{projectName}}

Deploys {{projectName}} to **AWS EKS** with **Terraform** (infrastructure) and
**Helm** (the application). Everything is environment-scoped and driven by
`dude iac …`, which works identically on your laptop and in CI/CD.

```
iac/
├── terraform/
│   ├── versions.tf            # provider + S3 backend (partial)
│   ├── providers.tf           # aws / kubernetes / helm (exec auth)
│   ├── variables.tf           # all inputs (sane defaults)
│   ├── main.tf                # VPC + EKS + ECR + ALB controller{{#if withPostgres}} + RDS{{/if}}
│   ├── outputs.tf             # cluster name, ECR URLs{{#if withPostgres}}, RDS endpoint{{/if}}
│   ├── bootstrap/             # one-time: S3 state bucket + DynamoDB lock table
│   └── environments/
│       └── dev/
│           ├── backend.hcl        # remote-state location for this env
│           └── terraform.tfvars   # this env's sizing/knobs
└── helm/
    └── app/                   # the application chart (backend, frontend,{{#if withRedis}} redis,{{/if}}{{#if withCelery}} celery,{{/if}} ingress…)
```

## What gets provisioned

- **VPC** across 3 AZs (public + private{{#if withPostgres}} + database{{/if}} subnets), NAT gateway.
- **EKS** cluster + a managed node group.
- **ECR** repositories for the backend and frontend images.
- **AWS Load Balancer Controller** (for ALB Ingress), wired via IRSA.
{{#if withPostgres}}- **RDS PostgreSQL** (managed); the master password is stored in AWS Secrets Manager.
{{/if}}- **Redis**{{#if withRedis}} runs **in-cluster** via the Helm chart{{else}} is not used{{/if}}.

The application itself (backend, frontend,{{#if withCelery}} Celery worker{{#if withCeleryBeat}}, beat{{/if}}, Flower,{{/if}}{{#if withRedis}} Redis,{{/if}} Ingress) is deployed by the Helm chart.

## Prerequisites

- `terraform` ≥ 1.6, `kubectl`, `helm`, and the `aws` CLI on your PATH.
- AWS credentials configured (`aws configure` or environment variables / SSO).

## One-time: remote state

Terraform state lives in S3 with a DynamoDB lock — created once per project:

```bash
cd iac/terraform/bootstrap
terraform init
terraform apply -var project_name={{projectName}} -var region=eu-west-1 -var state_bucket_prefix=<your-org>
```

Copy the two outputs (`state_bucket`, `lock_table`) into
`environments/dev/backend.hcl` (replace the `CHANGE-ME-…` placeholders).

## Provision an environment

```bash
dude iac init   --env dev      # terraform init (configures the S3 backend)
dude iac plan   --env dev      # review changes
dude iac apply  --env dev      # create/update infrastructure
dude iac output --env dev      # cluster name, ECR URLs{{#if withPostgres}}, RDS endpoint{{/if}}
```

## Build & push images (CI or local)

Image build/push is intentionally left to you / CI so it fits your pipeline.
Repos and URLs come from `dude iac output`:

```bash
REGISTRY=$(dude iac output --env dev --json | jq -r '.ecr_backend_repository_url.value' | cut -d/ -f1)
aws ecr get-login-password --region eu-west-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin "$REGISTRY"

# Backend (production image)
docker build -f backend/Dockerfile.prod -t "$REGISTRY/{{projectName}}-backend:<tag>" backend
docker push "$REGISTRY/{{projectName}}-backend:<tag>"

# Frontend (production image — nginx)
docker build -f frontend/Dockerfile.prod --build-arg VITE_APP_TITLE="{{projectName}}" \
  -t "$REGISTRY/{{projectName}}-frontend:<tag>" frontend
docker push "$REGISTRY/{{projectName}}-frontend:<tag>"
```

## Deploy the application

```bash
dude iac kubeconfig --env dev    # point kubectl/helm at the cluster
```

Set the image registry{{#if withPostgres}} and database URL{{/if}} in `helm/app/values-dev.yaml`
(or pass `--set` flags), then:

```bash
dude iac deploy --env dev        # helm upgrade --install
dude iac status --env dev        # release status + pods
```

{{#if withPostgres}}`secrets.databaseUrl` must be set (the chart refuses to deploy otherwise). Build it from
`dude iac output rds_endpoint` and the password in Secrets Manager
(`rds_master_user_secret_arn`).

{{/if}}Find the public URL:

```bash
kubectl get ingress -n dev
```

## Add a new environment

It scales by copying a folder — no code changes:

```bash
cp -r iac/terraform/environments/dev iac/terraform/environments/prod
cp iac/helm/app/values-dev.yaml iac/helm/app/values-prod.yaml
```

Then edit `environments/prod/{backend.hcl,terraform.tfvars}` (new state `key`,
bigger nodes,{{#if withPostgres}} `db_multi_az = true`, `db_deletion_protection = true`,{{/if}} …) and
`values-prod.yaml`, and run every command with `--env prod`.

## Local vs CI/CD

The exact same commands run in CI — state is remote (S3) and auth is via the
AWS CLI exec plugin, so no kubeconfig file is committed. A pipeline typically
runs: `dude iac init/plan/apply` → build & push images → `dude iac deploy`.

## Tear down

```bash
helm uninstall {{projectName}} -n dev
dude iac destroy --env dev
```

## Commands

| Command | Wraps |
|---------|-------|
| `dude iac init`      | `terraform init` (S3 backend per env) |
| `dude iac plan`      | `terraform plan` |
| `dude iac apply`     | `terraform apply` (`--yes` to auto-approve) |
| `dude iac destroy`   | `terraform destroy` (`--yes` to auto-approve) |
| `dude iac output`    | `terraform output` (`--json`) |
| `dude iac fmt`       | `terraform fmt -recursive` |
| `dude iac validate`  | `terraform validate` |
| `dude iac kubeconfig`| `aws eks update-kubeconfig` from outputs |
| `dude iac deploy`    | `helm upgrade --install` |
| `dude iac status`    | `helm status` + `kubectl get pods` |

All commands take `--env <name>` (default `dev`).
